WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
A 2014 case series found that 9 of 10 patients with cognitive impairment improved after a personalized metabolic and lifestyle program. This is Planetterrian Daily, episode 180. The approach combines several metabolic adjustments and daily habits, instead of focusing on one medication alone. 10 individuals diagnosed with Alzheimer's disease, mild cognitive impairment, or subjective cognitive impairment joined the effort. Within three to six months,

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:59.960
nine of them recorded either noticeable personal gains, or measurable test improvements. Six participants who had left their jobs or faced declining output, returned to work or handled tasks more effectively. The one person who stayed unchanged had reached a very advanced stage of Alzheimer's disease. The team called the effort a modest early test, and noted that bigger trials should follow. From multi-factor approaches to aging-related decline, we turned to a specific cellular

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:16.640
mechanism behind age-related muscle weakness. Researchers traced how disrupted calcium handling inside skeletal muscle cells produces lasting drops in strength. The work connects a basic cellular routine directly to everyday functional losses seen in older adults.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:43.920
Calcium normally triggers muscle contraction, yet repeated mishandling of the ion adds to weakness across time. The southern region of the Democratic Republic of Congo is known as the copper belt because of its rich deposits of copper and other minerals. A study found that cobalt exports from this area also contain unreported uranium. The presence of uranium in those exports raises questions about global nuclear risk tracking.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:02:04.740
London has one of the lowest violent crime rates of any region in England and Wales. It tops a global ranking of the best cities and pays more in taxes than it receives back in public spending. A new study from the Policy Institute at King's College London shows that the public overestimates how likely Londoners are to become victims of crime.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:24.220
The same study shows that Britain's underestimate the capital's international standing and have little idea it is the key contributor to public finances. A new database compiled by light and researchers tracks food-related emissions across cities, switching to sustainable diets could have emissions in places such as Amsterdam.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:31.600
In cities such as DACA and Delhi, the priority lies in making production more sustainable while widening access.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:37.680
The database shows that different cities need different approaches to achieve a more sustainable food system.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:47.860
Researchers at Umea University showed that even highly antibiotic resistant bacteria become more vulnerable when their cell wall transport system is disrupted.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:55.920
The discovery reveals a previously overlooked weakness that could help researchers develop new strategies to combat antibiotic resistance.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:04.120
Cellular mechanisms of aging lead naturally to another basic bacterial process that persists even in resistant strains.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:13.040
Umea University scientists showed that blocking a conserved transport system leaves even multi-drug resistant bacteria open to attack.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:18.360
The method strikes at a shared cellular step rather than any single resistance gene.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:24.560
Because the target stays common across strains, the tactic may slow the rise of further resistance.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:30.600
Additional tests in animal models and varied clinical samples will check how well the effect holds.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:37.800
From bacterial vulnerabilities we move to how bacteria themselves store and use information from past environments.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:44.740
Most people assume that storing and acting on past experience requires neurons or at least a nervous system.

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:52.920
Bacterial populations keep records of earlier nutrient levels or stress signals through shifts in protein amounts and feedback loops.

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:59.320
These records pass across several generations and let cells react more quickly when similar conditions return.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:07.240
Populations in soil, water, and the human body adjust their behavior based on conditions encountered hours or days earlier.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:16.180
The same principle of holding information in stable molecular states works at the single cell scale and can be examined directly in laboratory settings.

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:23.480
Researchers continue to identify the exact molecular switches that update or clear these records when surroundings shift.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:27.860
Gene editing alone does not produce a full plant from edited cells.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:37.100
The new CRISPR combo approach improves the speed and reliability of growing complete plants from edited cells in hard to breed perennial species.

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:42.940
Editing a plant's genes is only half the battle before a new trait can reach a farmer's field.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:50.900
An international team including CIIMAR developed a new genomic resource for the Japanese clam and the good clam.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:58.820
The tool supports selection for disease resistance and climate tolerance while helping maintain genetic diversity in aquaculture stocks.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:02.020
The study was published in the journal aquaculture.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:10.820
Researchers at King Abdullah University of Science and Technology led a global assessment of oysters, muscles, crabs, and similar species.

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:16.100
The study supplies one of the most detailed pictures yet of their contribution to human nutrition.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:20.820
The findings also inform sustainable fisheries planning and aquaculture.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:30.940
Scientists from Universitat Polytechnica de Barcelona and the ESRF used synchrotron analysis to trace the chemical reactions inside nanoparticles.

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:35.840
Those reactions created shimmering effects on 9th century Islamic ceramics.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:38.540
The results appear in science advances.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:45.160
From protein water interactions we shift to how motor proteins coordinate movement along cellular tracks.

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:50.980
New imaging methods now display the layout of water molecules that sit around protein surfaces.

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:56.540
That surrounding arrangement appears to shape how proteins carry out their roles inside cells.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:04.440
For decades attention stayed on a amino acid order and overall protein shape, yet the water layer adds another layer of detail.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:10.700
Coordinated molecular motion leads to population level genetic outcomes shaped by mating patterns.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:18.020
Supercomputer runs map the three-dimensional shape of the Kinesan neck region and its contacts with microtubules.

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:24.660
The next section proves essential for keeping the two motor domains in step so the protein stays on its track.

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:30.020
Kinesan moves along microtubules by timing the action of its paired motor domains.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:37.760
University of East Anglia work found that offspring carry fewer harmful genetic changes when females mate with several males.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:44.860
The drop in damaging mutations supports better health across the group and reduces the chance of population loss.

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:50.400
Multiple mating therefore supplies one root toward greater resistance against extinction pressures.

